.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The SSH binary packet protocol (RFC 4253 section 6), for the AEAD ciphers of the policy.
.PP
A gf_packet holds the current read and write protection state and nothing else: no buffers, no allocation.  Before the first NEWKEYS a direction is plaintext (the key exchange itself travels unprotected); afterwards every packet is sealed by one of
.PP
.nf
.RS
chacha20\-poly1305@openssh.com   (OpenSSH PROTOCOL.chacha20poly1305:
                                 the length is encrypted too, by a
                                 second ChaCha20 key)
aes256\-gcm@openssh.com, aes128\-gcm@openssh.com
                                (RFC 5647: the length is the
                                 associated data and travels in the
                                 clear)
.RE
.fi
.PP
Installing new keys is how NEWKEYS is expressed.  With strict key exchange (see policy.h) the sequence number of the direction is reset to zero then; the sequence number otherwise runs for the whole connection, and no direction may let it wrap: when it reaches 2^32 \- 1 protection and parsing both fail with GF_ERR_RANGE, so a connection must rekey long before (conn.h does).
.PP
Reading is a push parser in the style of the rest of the library: hand it a buffer holding the stream so far; it consumes whole packets, decrypting them in place, and stops at a partial packet (GF_NEED_MORE, buffer left at the start of that packet).  A packet is all\-or\-nothing: nothing is delivered before the tag has been verified.  The length field is checked as soon as it is readable, so a bogus length is refused before any of the packet it announces has to arrive.  Each packet is delivered as an event: a slice of the (now plaintext) buffer holding the payload, without padding.  The callback may install new keys with gf_packet_set_keys; they apply from the next packet.
.PP
Writing takes one payload and produces one packet, with random padding.
